 ],
 "answer_text": "Found 5 pubmed records: On tree longevity. [4], Tree mycorrhizal type and tree diversity shape the forest soil microbiota. [5], Gradient Boosted Tree Approaches for Mapping European Organization for Research and Treatment of Cancer Quality of Life Questionnaire Core 30 Onto 5-Level Version of EQ-5D Index for Patients With Cancer. [6], Tree diversity effects on productivity depend on mycorrhizae and life strategies in a temperate forest experiment. [7] and Seasonal patterns of nonstructural carbohydrate storage and mobilization in two tree species with distinct life-history traits. [8]. \n\nMycorrhizal type, diversity, and soil microbiota\n\nMycorrhizal type significantly affects fungal but not bacterial alpha diversity, and higher tree diversity promotes convergence of soil microbial communities [1]. Ectomycorrhizal but not arbuscular mycorrhizal tree species pairs showed increased fungal alpha diversity with greater tree diversity, and differences between the two types disappeared in multi-species mixtures [1]. \n\nMycorrhizae and biodiversity\u2013productivity relationships\n\nEctomycorrhizal communities showed low initial productivity that increased over time with strong biodiversity effects, whereas arbuscular mycorrhizal communities displayed the opposite pattern [2]. \n\nNonstructural carbohydrate storage and mobilization\n\nMassoniana, respectively [3]. \n\nPubmed records found\n\nPubmed title: On tree longevity [4]. Pubmed title: Tree mycorrhizal type and tree diversity shape the forest soil microbiota [5]. Pubmed title: Gradient Boosted Tree Approaches for Mapping European Organization for Research and Treatment of Cancer Quality of Life Questionnaire Core 30 Onto 5-Level Version of EQ-5D Index for Patients With Cancer [6]. Pubmed title: Tree diversity effects on productivity depend on mycorrhizae and life strategies in a temperate forest experiment [7]. Pubmed title: Seasonal patterns of nonstructural carbohydrate storage and mobilization in two tree species with distinct life-history traits [8]. "
